steps.body:
A union of string literals is the simplest replacement: `type Level = "low" | "medium" | "high"`. Callers pass the strings themselves, the checker rejects any other string, and the editor offers the three as completions. When you also need the values at runtime, to loop over them or check input, keep them in an `as const` array and derive the type from it, so the list and the type can't drift apart.

When code wants names like `Color.Red`, pair an `as const` object with a type of the same name:

```typescript
const Color = { Red: "red", Green: "green" } as const;
type Color = (typeof Color)[keyof typeof Color];

const favorite: Color = Color.Green;
console.log(favorite); // green
```

A value and a type can share a name because TypeScript keeps them apart: in `favorite: Color` the name means the type, and in `Color.Green` it means the object. Unlike a string enum, the type also accepts the plain string, so `const c: Color = "red"` compiles.

